00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.490
All right, well,
before we get stuck

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.704
into the under the hood section,
we have done several episodes--

00:05:50.704 --> 00:05:52.370
I think I'm actually
counting about six,

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:53.661
so i went back and had a look--

00:05:53.661 --> 00:05:56.680
where we discussed
BigQuery itself.

00:05:56.680 --> 00:05:59.040
But for those people who
may not have listened

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.880
or just want a refresher, what--

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.500
like very high level--
what is BigQuery?

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.795
Let's do like the three
minute sort of thing.

00:06:05.795 --> 00:06:06.920
TINO TERESHKO: So BigQuery.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:10.170
BigQuery is, in very
simplistic terms,

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:12.970
is a serverless, fully managed--

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:14.580
whatever buzzword
you want to use--

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.880
high level of
automation service that

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.610
allows you to store
vast amounts of data

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.920
or little amounts of
data for analytics

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:27.220
and then perform analytics
on that data in a SQL format.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.780
So that's in very,
very simple terms.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.040
But when you actually
start peeling the onion,

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.570
you see that there is a whole
lot of stuff behind BigQuery,

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:41.790
the compute engine of BigQuery,
the thing that executes SQL,

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:46.860
is actually Dremel, which is
this internal service that

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.300
is ubiquitous at Google.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.150
And then we have this
fantastic storage engine.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.870
It's all tied together
for networking.

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:57.630
And really, the secret glue
that nobody ever talks about,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.740
is our scheduler and
the type of magic

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:03.870
that's possible to
sling resources around

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.244
from query to query.

00:07:05.244 --> 00:07:06.160
That's essentially it.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.290
But our customers really
appreciate the simplicity

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.050
of the product and how easy
it is to scale up and down.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.630
And I think, really, the
biggest thing, again,

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:20.120
comes down to the fact
that it truly is no ops.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:20.940
Its no hassle.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.390
You set then forget
BigQuery, and you just

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.140
focus on things that
are important to you.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:25.620
JORDAN TIGANI:
Yeah, I would just

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.720
add that one way
of thinking of it

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.342
is a Cloud based
no-ops data warehouse.

00:07:32.342 --> 00:07:33.900
And it's a lot simpler--

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:36.840
simpler to set up and
certainly simpler to scale--

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:37.964
than other options.

00:07:37.964 --> 00:07:38.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.150
So you mentioned something about
Dremel, which is kind of cool,

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:45.330
because I know that Dremel
is a research paper.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:48.060
Can you talk a little
bit about how BigQuery

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.070
came to be since Dremel?

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.250
Is it the same thing
just like made available

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.380
to people outside of
Google, or is there

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.760
any differences in there?

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.801
JORDAN TIGANI: It's sort
of an interesting story,

00:07:58.801 --> 00:08:02.102
and we kind of didn't
used to talk about it,

00:08:02.102 --> 00:08:04.560
basically because there were
some kind of internal politics

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.530
things around it.

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.090
But essentially a bunch of
us in the Seattle office

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.700
had gotten pulled
off of other products

00:08:11.700 --> 00:08:15.090
that we were working on because
the site director wanted

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:18.560
us to build a data marketplace.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.590
And so we all got together
in the dining room

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:24.717
of the original
tech lead and tried

00:08:24.717 --> 00:08:27.300
to figure out how are we going
to build this data marketplace.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.180
And what we realized was that
you wanted more than like--

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:34.314
in order to be able to
sell large data sets--

00:08:34.314 --> 00:08:35.730
and you know we're
Google, we have

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:40.419
to deal in large data, that's
sort of what we do best--

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.690
is you wanted more than just
sort of a download link.

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:43.870
And you also kind of--

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:46.800
there's this concept that
when you have large data sets,

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.890
you need to bring the
compute to the data,

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:51.850
rather than the
data to the compute.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.240
So what we decided
was we would build

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.240
something that would
allow you to bring

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:58.540
the compute to the data.

00:08:58.540 --> 00:09:01.170
So you'd give us your data.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.350
The data would sort
of sit in our cloud,

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.820
and then you would
run your computations.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:10.680
And we harness the
internal Dremel engine

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.770
in order to make this happen.

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:16.230
And sort of six years later,
we haven't gotten around

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:18.230
to the whole data
marketplace thing.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:22.080
We might someday, but this
turned into a quite successful,

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.040
successful product.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.540
TINO TERESHKO: If
I may add to that,

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:28.590
I think it is true that BigQuery
does sit on top of Dremel

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:32.030
in terms of the execution
part and scheduling.

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:35.460
I think the main
difference is that we

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:38.670
can treat Googlers
much worse than we

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.250
can treat external customers.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:45.030
We can pop them into all
kinds of crazy, weird dog food

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.840
that we can't do with customers
that pay us real money.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:52.560
And that's kind of, I think,
been historically the way.

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:54.870
For example, the
component of Dremel

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.330
that's really undergone
really dramatic change

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.235
is the actual execution engine.

00:09:59.235 --> 00:10:02.709
What's in the papers, no longer
really the case these days.

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:04.250
The new engine called
Dremel X that's

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:06.500
been live for about
two years in production

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:08.780
was really dogfooded
for a long time,

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:11.990
internally, before the
team was comfortable enough

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:16.640
to kind of thrust that
onto the rest of the world.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.230
And the average customer,
the average user of BigQuery,

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:22.827
doesn't really know when they
switched over to new engine.

00:10:22.827 --> 00:10:24.910
They just kind of ran a
query, and all of a sudden

00:10:24.910 --> 00:10:26.360
it was five times faster.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.400
And that's really
how they knew they

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.440
were on the new stuff
instead of the old stuff.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:31.760
MARK MANDEL: This
sounds really cool.

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:33.510
But let's maybe take
a step back from some

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:36.302
of the words like Dremel and
stuff that people don't know.

00:10:36.302 --> 00:10:37.260
What does it look like?

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.580
So say I've got, I don't know,
a terabyte or two of data and I

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.050
write some SQL.

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:46.790
How does the magic happen that
turns that SQL into computation

00:10:46.790 --> 00:10:49.100
that potentially spans
across multiple computers

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.150
and does lots of crazy things?

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:53.330
Can you talk us, kind of,
through that step by step?

00:10:53.330 --> 00:10:54.163
JORDAN TIGANI: Sure.

00:10:54.163 --> 00:10:57.470
It's a little hard to do that
without diagrams and just

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:01.540
by kind of describing the data
flow, but one way, I think,

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:04.610
of describing the
Dremel execution engine

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:08.600
is there's a query master,
and there's a bunch of shards.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.440
And the query master
gets the query.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:15.260
It parses it, figures
out a query plan,

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:18.080
works with the scheduler,
schedules execution

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.850
of the different
parts of the query,

00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:22.790
and then schedules a whole
bunch of these shards

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:24.590
to execute that query.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:28.370
One of the nice things about
SQL is it's very parallelizable.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:33.020
Where clauses and filters can
all be completely parallelized.

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:36.260
Aggregations can be
partially parallelized.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.420
And they can be
completely parallelized

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:40.490
if you employ a shuffle.

00:11:40.490 --> 00:11:43.160
So we have a really fast
in-memory shuffle that

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:44.690
also gets into the process.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.670
The original Dremel,
the Dremel paper,

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:48.919
was sort of a tree
shape, where, kind

00:11:48.919 --> 00:11:50.960
of, the filters would
happen at the lowest level,

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:53.400
the aggregations would
sort of bubble up the tree.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:56.330
But this didn't really
work for complex queries,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:58.160
where you kind of
would need to traverse

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.480
the tree multiple times.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:02.210
And so now what happens is
rather than a static tree,

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:07.010
the query master will kind
of design a multi-level tree,

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:11.692
whereas in between we used a
fast in-memory shuffle or we,

00:12:11.692 --> 00:12:13.400
in some cases, where
we can directly pass

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:16.274
the data to the next stage.

00:12:16.274 --> 00:12:18.440
And this allows the queries
to be much more flexible

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:22.392
and allows us to be flexible
in how we allocate resources.

00:12:22.392 --> 00:12:24.350
TINO TERESHKO: Let me
add a little bit of sugar

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:24.980
from my side.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:26.990
So you heard kind of like
the engineering point,

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:29.420
and now maybe the
customer facing point

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.070
that I like to think about is.

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.360
Imagine you have this big old--

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.630
those IBM blue
supercomputers that

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:37.940
used to be famous in
the '80s and the '90s.

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:41.600
Imagine if you had terminal
access to one of those,

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.410
and you could just rent one of
those for four or five seconds

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:48.915
at a time and just pay
per second, essentially.

00:12:48.915 --> 00:12:50.540
That's kind of what
BigQuery gives you,

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:54.050
like, BigQuery gives you
access to this incredibly

00:12:54.050 --> 00:12:58.550
vast supercomputer that Google
manages for you called Dremel.

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.430
And you push the
button, and you say,

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:05.090
I want to use this thing
for a few seconds at a time.

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:06.120
That's pretty awesome.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:07.036
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:07.036 --> 00:13:10.370
So now I'm wondering, you
saying that there is basically

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:12.627
a lot of computers
running this that

00:13:12.627 --> 00:13:14.960
even though it looks like
there's only one big computer.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:18.000
What is going on in
this basically massively

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.530
parallelized query that
is sent all around?

00:13:21.530 --> 00:13:24.110
And I guess that how
that is actually done,

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:27.570
like that is into the details of
the paper and stuff like that.

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:32.520
But I'm curious about how, like,
how does this actually work?

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.685
Like, do you need to have
like a cluster of computers,

00:13:35.685 --> 00:13:38.060
like when Kubernetes, for
instance, you create a cluster,

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:41.050
and then you're ready
to start running things?

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:42.105
Does it work like that?

00:13:42.105 --> 00:13:43.480
JORDAN TIGANI: As
Tino mentioned,

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:45.860
it's a multi-tenant system.

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:51.440
So it's essentially we host
a number of giant computing

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:56.240
clusters, and everybody's
queries kind of get time sliced

00:13:56.240 --> 00:14:00.830
onto some of these clusters.

00:14:00.830 --> 00:14:07.010
And we strive to give every
user 2000 slots, which basically

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:10.280
means 2000 units of
individually schedulable action

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:12.860
that they can run in
parallel, so essentially 2000

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:14.780
shards per user.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:19.100
You might not get that if you
query in a very popular time,

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:24.860
but you should nearly always
get that much execution power.

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:28.550
And that's going to only be
a very small fraction of any

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:32.120
of our Dremel trees, and we
have a number of Dremel trees

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:37.187
that are around the world,
mostly in the US and in Europe.

00:14:37.187 --> 00:14:39.020
TINO TERESHKO: And I'll
add to that as well.

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:41.390
Jordan was talking about
just the compute component,

00:14:41.390 --> 00:14:42.860
the query, Dremel.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.430
Unlike any kind of
traditional data warehouse,

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:47.870
or data lake product, or
whatever you want to call that,

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:50.717
BigQuery does have separation,
storage, and compute,

00:14:50.717 --> 00:14:52.550
which tends to be a
popular term these days.

00:14:52.550 --> 00:14:56.560
So query is entirely
separate from storage.

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:59.510
So storage is unlimited,
it's fully managed,

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:01.200
it's really, really inexpensive.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:04.500
But you can leverage
analytic capacity

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.750
on top of that stories
in very elastic ways.

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:10.080
But also what BigQuery
has that's really unique

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:14.580
is we also separate compute
from intermediate state.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:19.560
So a lot of times, in a
typical similar technology,

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:23.210
keeps state in the nodes that
are processing themselves.

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:24.640
BigQuery does that.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:26.640
But BigQuery reserves
the right to keep state

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:29.880
and what Jordan referred to
as this separate in-memory

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:30.452
shuffler.

00:15:30.452 --> 00:15:32.910
This allows us to do all kinds
of really interesting things

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:34.870
when it comes to
efficiency and performance.

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:36.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
now I'm curious about--

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:39.180
you're talking about the
compute set of things

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.647
and then the storage
set of things.

00:15:40.647 --> 00:15:42.480
And for the compute set
of things, basically

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:44.771
what we're doing is we're
running-- rather than running

00:15:44.771 --> 00:15:48.499
one really big query, there
is thousands of them, like,

00:15:48.499 --> 00:15:49.540
or I don't know how many.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:51.630
But many, many of those
little jobs running

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:53.930
all around that big query tree.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:55.740
How are those managed?

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:57.450
We had an episode on Borg.

00:15:57.450 --> 00:15:58.680
Is this running on Borg?

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:00.060
Is this running on Kubernetes?

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:01.061
Can you talk about this?

00:16:01.061 --> 00:16:01.851
JORDAN TIGANI: Yes.

00:16:01.851 --> 00:16:03.150
So they're running on Borg.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:08.250
We also have our own scheduler
to sort of deal with when--

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:10.126
to redispatch queries
that, you know, maybe--

00:16:10.126 --> 00:16:11.875
or part of a query was
running on a shard,

00:16:11.875 --> 00:16:12.810
and that shard dies--

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:16.230
to recognize that and
redispatch it elsewhere.

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.750
TINO TERESHKO: Yeah,
the scheduler also

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:22.050
kind of makes you immune from
any kind of individual hardware

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:23.081
downtime.

00:16:23.081 --> 00:16:25.080
The scheduler knows when
something is happening,

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:27.330
and we'll try to reparallelize
the workload and things

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:28.270
like that.

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:30.144
JORDAN TIGANI: And Borg
is what's responsible

00:16:30.144 --> 00:16:34.170
for-- if a machine dies or
if one of the shards dies--

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.895
it'll restart it and
rejoin the cluster.

00:16:36.895 --> 00:16:39.270
TINO TERESHKO: One distinction
I want to make here before

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:45.510
[INAUDIBLE] is any kind of
typical similar technology,

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:47.490
when you stand up a cluster--

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:49.680
quote, unquote--
you essentially have

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.840
a process that is really fast.

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.674
Automatically, it
goes out and puts out

00:16:53.674 --> 00:16:55.590
sticky notes on a bunch
of hardware that says,

00:16:55.590 --> 00:16:58.590
this is my hardware right now.

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:03.720
And BigQuery does that per
job very, very quickly.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:06.630
This process that puts
sticky notes on hardware

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:10.176
executes in less than a
second at massive scale.

00:17:10.176 --> 00:17:12.300
And then once the query is
done, all those stickies

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:13.440
are thrown away.

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:17.030
So it's per job rather
than per getting

00:17:17.030 --> 00:17:18.599
a cluster going type of thing.

00:17:18.599 --> 00:17:19.440
That's pretty cool.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:19.800
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.308
And something you
definitely alluded

00:17:21.308 --> 00:17:22.349
to earlier talking about.

00:17:22.349 --> 00:17:24.224
So we've been talking
about the compute side,

00:17:24.224 --> 00:17:26.160
and sort of on the
storage side, is there

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:29.850
anything particularly special
about how BigQuery stores

00:17:29.850 --> 00:17:33.180
the data that enables it to
parallelize in a great way

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:34.920
or able to affect
the performance?

00:17:34.920 --> 00:17:35.680
Or is it--

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:36.750
I'm just going to
assume it's not

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:38.850
just a hard drive sitting on
someone's computer under a desk

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:39.540
somewhere

00:17:39.540 --> 00:17:42.870
JORDAN TIGANI: So we use our
own proprietary columnar storage

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:44.700
format called Capacitor.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:47.760
We looked at a bunch
of open column formats,

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.090
including Parquet, which
is really, really common

00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:52.917
in the open source community.

00:17:52.917 --> 00:17:54.750
And there wasn't anything
that would give us

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:58.890
enough control over how
the queries are executed.

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:01.730
For example, in some
of the metadata,

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:04.830
it's arranged in a way
that the query engine has

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:08.730
to do very little work
in order to satisfy

00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:11.040
a large part of the queries.

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:13.890
The hash tables that we built
were sort of already pre-built

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.410
in those headers,
and we followed

00:18:16.410 --> 00:18:19.920
a lot of research papers and
showed us some state of the art

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:24.900
ideas that people had
and productionized it

00:18:24.900 --> 00:18:28.110
into our storage format.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:32.340
So yeah, each column is
stored in a separate chunk

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:36.270
of the file, and this
allows many databases

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:37.740
to use column storage.

00:18:37.740 --> 00:18:43.260
Now I think we have a pretty
advanced version of that

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.050
that builds on top of the sort
of the standard column store

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:50.530
and allows us to A, compress
better and B, read less data.

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:52.530
MARK MANDEL: So I'll ask
the pertinent question.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:54.989
What does a column
store actually give you?

00:18:54.989 --> 00:18:56.280
Like, what's the benefit there?

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:59.820
JORDAN TIGANI: If you
imagine a record store, where

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:01.230
you store a full
record, and then

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:03.900
the-- the first record
and the next record,

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.520
and then you try
to compress that,

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:07.145
that's not going to
compress very well,

00:19:07.145 --> 00:19:10.080
because compression works
on eliminating redundancy.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.510
And the redundancy that
you have across a row

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:14.910
is pretty small in general.

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:17.970
But usually, in a column,
there's a lot of redundancy.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.359
Maybe you have telephone
numbers and the prefix

00:19:20.359 --> 00:19:21.150
is always the same.

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:24.450
Or maybe you have countries, and
there's only like 10 countries

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:25.540
that you deal with.

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:29.680
And so these tend to
compress very nicely.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:31.860
The other thing you
can do is, especially

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:35.460
if you're using a distributed
file system where you're not

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:38.160
worried about the speed
of one spinning disk,

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.160
is that you can read--

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:43.530
in a column store, you can read
different columns in parallel,

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:45.030
because they will end up--

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:47.850
under the hood, they will be
stored on different disks.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:52.030
So you get a faster
effective I/O throughput

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:54.284
by reading these multiple
columns at the same time.

00:19:54.284 --> 00:19:55.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:56.820
And I guess that
this is also why,

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.250
from the point of
view of a customer,

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:03.400
BigQuery doesn't charge you for
the columns you do not read.

00:20:03.400 --> 00:20:05.380
It is because you
actually do not read them,

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:07.297
so there's no compute
cost to send it to them.

00:20:07.297 --> 00:20:08.463
JORDAN TIGANI: Yes, exactly.

00:20:08.463 --> 00:20:11.440
So yeah, you save on reading the
columns you don't care about.

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:14.590
And in fact, yeah, that was
one of the original reasons

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:16.510
that Dremel was created,
is because there

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:19.180
was an engineer who
was doing a lot of map

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:22.600
produces over these log
files, and these log files

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:25.570
had actually hundreds
of thousands of columns.

00:20:25.570 --> 00:20:28.430
But he was only using a
couple of those columns.

00:20:28.430 --> 00:20:30.320
And so he was reading
all of this extra data.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:33.490
And so he just switched his
data to a columnar format

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.020
and wrote a little SQL
engine on top of it.

00:20:36.020 --> 00:20:38.500
And that's sort of
what then became Dremel

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:39.790
and then what became BigQuery.

00:20:39.790 --> 00:20:40.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So I have a story

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:43.085
that I heard that I don't
know if it's true or not.

00:20:43.085 --> 00:20:45.910
But I heard that
Dremel was created

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:49.240
when those map producers
that you were mentioning

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:52.690
were actually taking almost
24 hours to complete,

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.770
to process 24 hours on logs,
which is a bad place to be.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:21:00.010
Is that true or is it just like
a story that someone told me

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:01.637
just to brag a
little bit about it?

00:21:01.637 --> 00:21:03.220
JORDAN TIGANI: That's
absolutely true.

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:05.304
And the engineer who created
it was basically-- he

00:21:05.304 --> 00:21:07.511
created it while he was
waiting for his map producers

00:21:07.511 --> 00:21:08.336
to finish.

00:21:08.336 --> 00:21:10.210
TINO TERESHKO: It had
every major innovation.

00:21:10.210 --> 00:21:13.420
There is a very lazy engineer.

00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:15.590
MARK MANDEL: I love the
fact that you're like, yeah,

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:17.260
so he just put this
thing together.

00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:18.430
It's like a columnar thing.

00:21:18.430 --> 00:21:20.230
And then he put a SQL, like
it's just like yeah, it's fine.

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:22.355
He did it in like a couple
of afternoons, whatever.

00:21:22.355 --> 00:21:23.590
And I'm like, what?

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:26.070
JORDAN TIGANI: I think
that's just sort of a, like,

00:21:26.070 --> 00:21:30.420
an interesting way that stuff
often can develop at Google.

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:35.335
Not to plug Google too
heavily, but he started it.

00:21:35.335 --> 00:21:36.370
It wasn't a big deal.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.440
It was a tool that he
wrote, he wrote himself.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:41.650
And it required a lot of setup.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.230
And he hired him--

00:21:44.230 --> 00:21:47.200
they had an intern, and the
intern started working on it.

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:49.240
And then a couple of
the 20% project people

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:51.240
started working on it.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:55.660
And now we've got dozens of
engineers and Fortune 500

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:57.200
companies relying on it.

00:21:57.200 --> 00:21:58.658
TINO TERESHKO: And
they like to say

00:21:58.658 --> 00:22:01.460
that at Google, the
hard problems are easy,

00:22:01.460 --> 00:22:02.990
and the easy problems are hard.

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:06.640
So we built this massive
supercomputer engine

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.520
that just churns through data.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:11.590
But for the longest
time, we had people offer

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:13.729
into approximate counter
state, for example.

00:22:13.729 --> 00:22:15.520
And that was just kind
of, like, the nature

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:18.970
of this organic buildup.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:21.490
And because of Google
Cloud, we actually

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.000
have to care about customers.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:25.460
So now we have what
the standard calls for.

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:27.670
JORDAN TIGANI: And
yeah, it did lead

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:30.430
us to do some things
that, in retrospect, we

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:31.760
wouldn't have done.

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:33.550
Tino mentioned the
approx count to state,

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:36.700
where internally
folks were like,

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:40.180
well I'm doing logs
analysis, and I don't really

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:44.470
care if there's a million and
17 distinct users or a million

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:46.977
and 19 distinct users.

00:22:46.977 --> 00:22:49.060
They really all kind of
fit into that same bucket.

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:51.370
And the algorithm
to compute those

00:22:51.370 --> 00:22:54.910
is 10,000 times faster
to do it approximately,

00:22:54.910 --> 00:22:57.520
so I'm just going to
use the approximate one.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:23:00.900
And there are some syntax things
as well that we were like--

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:02.650
that made it easier
to process your logs,

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:07.280
but once we're trying to get
people who have been using SQL

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:10.660
all their lives to adopt it,
they found some frustrations.

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:13.970
Luckily, both of those
things have been fixed.

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:17.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So we've
talked so far about compute.

00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:19.960
We've talked a little bit about
storage, about how we store,

00:23:19.960 --> 00:23:20.840
but not where.

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:23.780
So I'm curious about where
do we store all of these?

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:25.520
Is it, like, local disks?

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:27.340
Is it in Google Cloud Storage?

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:31.172
JORDAN TIGANI: We store it in
the basement of building 42.

00:23:31.172 --> 00:23:32.380
MARK MANDEL: I've been there.

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:34.440
It's nice.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.300
JORDAN TIGANI: No, we
started in Colossus.

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:40.390
So Colossus is our
distributed file system.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:43.870
The Google Cloud Storage
also uses Colossus.

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:46.240
Things at Google tend to be--

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:47.950
new innovations tend
to be sort of built

00:23:47.950 --> 00:23:49.230
on top of older innovations.

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:51.897
So like the technology
stack tends to be very deep.

00:23:51.897 --> 00:23:53.980
Google Cloud Storage is
built on top of Blobstore,

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:56.770
which is built on top of
Colossus, which uses Bigtable,

00:23:56.770 --> 00:23:58.360
which uses Colossus.

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:01.900
And BigQuery stores
data on Colossus,

00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:05.660
and Colossus takes care
of transparent encryption.

00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.870
It takes care of encoding.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:10.330
We use an erasure encoding
so that the data is

00:24:10.330 --> 00:24:12.850
stored redundantly.

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:14.350
Basically there
are some checksums

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:15.470
that are stored elsewhere.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:18.180
So if there are failures that
happen on any particular disk,

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:21.460
in any particular chunk,
those can be recovered.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:23.410
And it's actually sort
of an elaborate kind

00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:27.520
of web of checksums that
can be used to recover

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:30.460
from lots of failures.

00:24:30.460 --> 00:24:33.010
And so our data
is actually stored

00:24:33.010 --> 00:24:36.190
quite durably within
a particular cell,

00:24:36.190 --> 00:24:39.460
and the cell you could
think of as a building.

00:24:39.460 --> 00:24:43.100
And then the data is replicated
to multiple locations.

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:45.705
So we replicate to a
couple of different cells,

00:24:45.705 --> 00:24:48.760
a couple of different buildings
within a region, actually.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:51.785
So the GC, the Google
Cloud terms or zones--

00:24:51.785 --> 00:24:53.410
we upgraded a couple
of different zones

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:55.600
within the region.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:25:00.250
And then we also store an
off region copy of the data

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.930
to make sure that in case
there's a fire, or a hurricane,

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:08.024
or something, that your data
is still durably stored.

00:25:08.024 --> 00:25:09.690
TINO TERESHKO: Again,
what line with how

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:13.290
BigQuery works-- you don't
know if any of that happens.

00:25:13.290 --> 00:25:15.600
It just kind of like--
it's a benefit you get.

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:16.100
And

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:18.830
JORDAN TIGANI: Also,
just to make sure,

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:21.915
we don't replicate your
data out of the country

00:25:21.915 --> 00:25:23.520
that you're storing
your data in.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:26.380
So if you say you're
storing your data in the EU,

00:25:26.380 --> 00:25:28.010
we don't replicate
it out of the EU.

00:25:28.010 --> 00:25:29.490
If you're storing
it in the US, we

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:30.990
don't replicate
it out of the US.

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:33.573
TINO TERESHKO: Here's one more
thing I want to say about that.

00:25:33.573 --> 00:25:35.790
Because Dremel is
this big thing that

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:38.520
has lots of compute
sitting around,

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:41.454
and sometimes it's
variable capacity demands

00:25:41.454 --> 00:25:42.120
on this compute.

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.450
Sometimes it's not 100%.

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:46.450
Actually, a lot of
times it's not 100%.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.520
So we essentially have excess
capacity on the compute set.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:53.190
So what sometimes
the storage engine

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:56.550
does is it looks at how the
data is stored physically

00:25:56.550 --> 00:26:01.110
inside of the query and says,
well, maybe it can be better.

00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:04.740
Maybe this table is driven
by 10 million small files,

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:08.490
and we can kind of coalesce
that number down to 10,000.

00:26:08.490 --> 00:26:11.520
Or maybe we can do some
other improvements here.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.490
And again, this also
happens without clients

00:26:14.490 --> 00:26:15.210
knowing about it.

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.492
You just kind of get
potentially better performance.

00:26:17.492 --> 00:26:18.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

