00:26:27.930 --> 00:26:32.430
to make all of this happen, for
real, you need a good network,

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:33.510
I'm going to assume?

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:35.670
Could you talk a little
bit about the importance

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:36.870
of the networking these.

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.914
Like, you're sending a
lot of data around, right?

00:26:39.914 --> 00:26:41.580
JORDAN TIGANI: There's
some bits in here

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:45.840
that are proprietary that
we don't usually talk about.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:50.610
But I think that one of the
real advantages that Google has

00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:53.610
is the quality of its
data center networking.

00:26:53.610 --> 00:26:59.310
It means you can move data
around anywhere within the cell

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:02.700
extremely quickly, and the
total throughput of that

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:08.430
is in the pet of
it range, which is

00:27:08.430 --> 00:27:11.340
pretty massive amount
of data, and it's also

00:27:11.340 --> 00:27:14.160
what lets us often
compete in performance

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:15.900
with in-memory databases.

00:27:15.900 --> 00:27:18.630
Because the kind of
performance that we get,

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:21.960
people would assume
you can't get with just

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:23.640
regular old spinning disks.

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:26.100
Because we were able
to A, parallelize it

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.650
into so many of
those disks and B,

00:27:28.650 --> 00:27:32.190
our network is so fast,
that the overall throughput

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:33.720
is extremely high.

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:38.646
And it's higher than you
could likely get elsewhere.

00:27:38.646 --> 00:27:39.520
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

