00:29:43.290 --> 00:29:45.510
So you need to know,
like, the basics like

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.260
for your transformer and stuff.

00:29:47.260 --> 00:29:47.440
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:29:47.440 --> 00:29:48.856
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Then you're going

00:29:48.856 --> 00:29:52.320
to need to develop probably
convolutional neural network--

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:54.710
maybe recurring, I'm not sure.

00:29:54.710 --> 00:29:58.567
to be able to understand that
to the speech recognition.

00:29:58.567 --> 00:30:00.900
And then from there, you need
to do the natural language

00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:04.005
processing, which also uses
convolutional neural networks,

00:30:04.005 --> 00:30:06.190
now actually more recurrent
neural networks then

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:06.940
convolutional.

00:30:06.940 --> 00:30:11.480
It's not super hard,
just like basic calculus.

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:12.634
So that is a way.

00:30:12.634 --> 00:30:14.800
And actually, if you want
to build it by yourselves,

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:15.839
you can totally do that.

00:30:15.839 --> 00:30:18.130
There's a different way,
which is to be more high level

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:21.880
and that I recommend, which
is to simply use Speech API.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:25.030
We have a natural
language processing API.

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:26.920
So those work really well.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:28.930
And you can build the
whole thing from scratch

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:30.160
if you want to.

00:30:30.160 --> 00:30:32.230
Or even better, you
can use something

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:35.680
that is designed specifically
for this use case, which

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:37.240
is api.ai.

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:39.490
And api.ai is a
super cool thing,

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:41.830
because it is a very
simple platform that

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:43.990
allows you to use machine
learning even though you

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:46.460
have no clue of how
machine learning works,

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:47.590
which is amazing.

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:49.100
MARK MANDEL: Because
I have no clue.

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:51.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I have
a little bit of a clue,

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:54.370
but I'm still very lost.

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:58.090
And basically what you do is
you define what we call intends.

00:30:58.090 --> 00:31:01.450
And those intends are much
in between the text you're

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:04.900
receiving and the
endpoints on your backend.

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:09.310
So at the end, what you develop
is basically just a rest API.

00:31:09.310 --> 00:31:12.585
And you're going to get like
JSON messages sent there,

00:31:12.585 --> 00:31:13.960
and you need to
respond to those.

00:31:13.960 --> 00:31:15.876
So for instance, if you
want to develop, like,

00:31:15.876 --> 00:31:18.730
I don't know, like a store,
you're going to have search,

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:20.830
and buy, and stuff like that.

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:22.602
And then you just give examples.

00:31:22.602 --> 00:31:24.560
So those examples, for
instance, you could say,

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:28.060
I would like to buy, I
don't know, Pixel XL.

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:30.490
When you say, I want to
buy a Pixel XL, what you're

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:34.710
going to say is Pixel XL is
an entity and I want to buy

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:35.830
is a use case.

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:37.780
And you just give a
bunch of examples,

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:41.310
like saying, well, if instead
of receiving, I want to buy,

00:31:41.310 --> 00:31:42.754
it was like I would like to get.

00:31:42.754 --> 00:31:44.170
Well, it's kind
of the same thing.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:46.870
So just give a bunch of
examples for the same intent.

00:31:46.870 --> 00:31:47.454
And that's it.

